comment.body:
From the trail side, **recovery geometry** is the section I would underline. Teams tend to test what the robot can drive over and not what two tired people can lift it out of. Before taking a platform onto public or semi-wild terrain, I would rehearse a dead-machine return from the farthest point: power isolated, wheels locked or free as designed, payload secured, and no assumption that a vehicle can reach it. The recovery equipment and route should be part of the mission envelope, not an improvised favor from whoever happens to be nearby.

The environmental boundary needs equal weight. “Outdoor” includes water, grit, needles, leaf litter, roots, reflective puddles, moving vegetation, wildlife, and people who did not consent to become test obstacles. I would add a field rule that the test pauses whenever the team cannot maintain a clear exclusion zone. The [Leave No Trace principles](https://lnt.org/why/7-principles/) are written for recreation, but planning ahead, using durable surfaces, respecting wildlife, and being considerate of others are good design constraints for robot fieldwork too.

Your capability ladder suggests a practical route progression:

1. repeatable indoor surface transitions;
2. a controlled outdoor pad with known slopes and edges;
3. a closed short route with planned recovery access;
4. a variable route with spotters and explicit stop points;
5. only then, a longer mission that combines autonomy and endurance.

I would log human impact alongside robot performance: interventions, off-route wheel tracks, disturbed soil, unexpected interactions, and whether the team left the site as found. That prevents “mission completed” from hiding a test that succeeded technically while creating a mess. It also gives future teams evidence about which surfaces and seasons are appropriate.

The final paragraph lands for me. Backpacking gear earns trust in the same unglamorous way: zippers that work with cold hands, a shelter that can be pitched in wind, a repair that can be made without a workbench, and a packing order that still makes sense in rain. **Dependability is capability plus a humane failure path.**
